
#117 — can the model's own rotating condensate supply the stiff (w >= 1) phase
that a BKL-safe contraction requires?

MATH_SPINE records the objection as "the recorded regimes do not supply w >= 1".
That is a survey statement. This is the calculation.

The model's founding identity is a charged ROTATING superfluid: a complex scalar
Psi = (r/sqrt2) e^{i theta} carrying a conserved charge Q = a^3 r^2 thetadot.
Eliminating thetadot at fixed Q turns the rotation into a centrifugal term in an
effective radial potential:

    V_eff(r) = V(r) + Q^2 / (2 a^6 r^2)

Two regimes are possible on approach:

  (A) TRACKING - r sits at the minimum of V_eff and slides as a shrinks.
  (B) FROZEN   - Hubble friction beats the restoring curvature, r stays put and
                 rho_rot = Q^2/(2 a^6 r^2) goes as a^-6, i.e. exactly kination.

(B) is the only way to get w = 1 out of this sector. So the question is entirely
"which regime holds on approach to the bounce", and that is decided by comparing
V_eff'' against H^2 at the tracking solution.

Result, both analytic and integrated below:
  * tracking in V ~ r^n gives  w = (n-2)/(n+2)  exactly -- strictly BELOW 1 for
    every polynomial n, approaching 1 only as n -> infinity.
  * the tracking solution is stable (so (B) never takes over) whenever the
    amplitude is sub-Planckian: V_eff''/H^2 = O(10) * (M_Pl/r)^2.

So the rotating sector cannot supply the stiff phase at any sub-Planckian
amplitude, for any polynomial potential. The BKL objection is not merely
unanswered by the recorded regimes -- it is unanswerable from this sector.

==========================================================================
TRACKING EQUATION OF STATE, V ~ r^n
==========================================================================
   n  w analytic  w sub-Planck  w trans-Planck   note
   2    0.000000      0.000064          1.0000   OK  quadratic - the mass era
   4    0.333333      0.333395          0.9998   OK  quartic - the recorded w=1/3 youth
   6    0.500000      0.500067          0.9988   OK  

  w = (n-2)/(n+2) -> 1 only as n -> infinity.
  No polynomial potential reaches w = 1. Kination is out of reach
  of the tracking rotating condensate, for every n.

==========================================================================
IS TRACKING ACTUALLY WHAT HAPPENS? (freeze would give w=1 instead)
==========================================================================
  Freezing needs Hubble friction to beat the restoring curvature,
  i.e. V_eff'' < H^2 (a few). At the tracking minimum:

     n         V_eff"/H^2   amplitude needed for r to freeze
     2      12 (M_Pl/r)^2   r > 3.5 M_Pl
     4      24 (M_Pl/r)^2   r > 4.9 M_Pl
     6      36 (M_Pl/r)^2   r > 6.0 M_Pl
    10      60 (M_Pl/r)^2   r > 7.7 M_Pl

  The ratio is (M_Pl/r)^2 up to O(10). For any SUB-PLANCKIAN
  amplitude the curvature dominates by orders of magnitude, the
  field tracks, and w is pinned at (n-2)/(n+2) < 1.

  VERDICT: the rotating sector cannot supply a BKL-safe stiff phase
  below the Planck scale. The objection is unanswerable from here,
  not merely unanswered.

==========================================================================
THE CONTRACTING BRANCH ON THE MODEL'S OWN (m, lambda) -- what eos it hits
==========================================================================
  Psi0 = m/sqrt(lambda) = 5.01e+25 eV = 4.1e-03 M_Pl (quartic=mass point)
     R/Psi0  h=lam R^2/m^2  local n        w
      1e+03       1.00e+06    4.000   0.3333
      1e+01       1.00e+02    3.980   0.3311
      1e+00       1.00e+00    3.000   0.2000
      1e-01       1.00e-02    2.020   0.0049
      1e-03       1.00e-06    2.000   0.0000

  The contracting branch passes through w = 1/3 (quartic youth) into
  w = 0 (mass era) -- the same sequence as expansion, reversed -- and
  never stiffens: the quartic=mass point Psi0 is itself sub-Planckian,
  and kination needs ~M_Pl amplitude that contraction does not reach
  before the bounce. So solving the field equation in time returns the
  SAME adverse BKL verdict; it does not rescue the bounce.
